          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Differential cohomology is a refinement of plain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            such that a differential cocycle is to its underlying ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycle"} cocycle
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+an+infinity-bundle"} bundle with connection
            is to its underlying
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+infinity-bundle"} bundle
          %p
            The best known version of differential cohomology is a differential refinement of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+%28Eilenberg-Steenrod%29+cohomology"} generalized (Eilenberg-Steenrod) cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+theory"} stable homotopy theory
            (as opposed to more general
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+cohomology"} nonabelian cohomology
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+differential+cohomology"} nonabelian differential cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
            the refinement to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+differential+cohomology"} ordinary differential cohomology
            is modeled for instance by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+line+bundles"} complex line bundles
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection on a bundle
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle+gerbes+with+connection"} bundle gerbes with connection
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne+cohomology"} Deligne cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+K-theory"} differential K-theory
            may be presented by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundles"} vector bundles
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} with connection
            (e.g
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#SimonsSullivan08"} Simons-Sullivan 08
          %p
            A systematic characterization and construction of differential
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+%28Eilenberg-Steenrod%29+cohomology"} generalized (Eilenberg-Steenrod) cohomology
            in terms of suitable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fiber+products"} homotopy fiber products
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+spectra"} mapping spectra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brown+representability+theorem"} representing
            the underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+theory"} cohomology theory
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential form
            data was then given in (
            %a{:href => "#HopkinsSinger02"}> Hopkins-Singer 02
            ) (motivated by discussion of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-brane"} M5-brane
            via holographically dual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/7d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 7d Chern-Simons theory
            by
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"} Edward Witten
          %p
            In this stable case one characteristic property of differential cohomology that was first observed empirically in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+differential+cohomology"} ordinary differential cohomology
            (
            %a{:href => "#SimonsSullivan07"}> Simons-Sullivan 07
            ) and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+K-theory"} differential K-theory
            (
            %a{:href => "#SimonsSullivan08"}> Simons-Sullivan 08
            ) is that it is a kind of cohomology theory which fits into a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology+diagram"} differential cohomology diagram
            which is an interlocking system of two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+sequences"} fiber sequences
            that expresses how the data of a geometric bundle with connection may be decomposed into the underlying bundle and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            of the connection and how connections on trivial bundles as well as bundles with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+infinity-connection"} flat connections
            are examples of the general concept. (The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fiber+product"} homotopy fiber product
            used in (
            %a{:href => "#HopkinsSinger02"}> Hopkins-Singer 02
            ) provides one of the two commuting squares in this diagram.)
          %p
            Schematically the characteristic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology+hexagon"} differential cohomology hexagon
            (see there for details) looks as follows
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi connection
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
