                  }
          %p
            It turns out that such
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology+diagrams"} differential cohomology diagrams
            are exactly what all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+types"} stable homotopy types
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} cohesive (∞,1)-toposes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            naturally sit in (
            %a{:href => "#BunkeNikolausVoelkl13"}> Bunke-Nikolaus-Völkl 13
            ). All the traditional differential cohomology theories and their joint generalization due to (
            %a{:href => "#HopkinsSinger02"}> Hopkins-Singer 02
            ) are special cases of this for the case that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo =
                  %mi T
                  %mi Smooth
                  %mn ∞
                  %mi Grpd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H} = T Smooth \infty Grpd
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+cohesive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} tangent cohesive (∞,1)-topos
            of that of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} smooth ∞-groupoids
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-stacks"} ∞-stacks
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
          %p
            It is therefore natural to
            %em define
            differential cohomology to be the intrinsic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} cohesive (∞,1)-toposes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology+diagram"} differential cohomology diagram
            itself only involves the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+modality"} shape modality
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+modality"} flat modality
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesion"} cohesion
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sharp+modality"} sharp modality
            is needed to produce, via
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+concretification"} differential concretification
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stacks"} moduli stacks
            of differential cocycles over a given base object from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+stack"} mapping stack
            of that into the representing
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+type"} stable homotopy type
          %p
            Viewed in this generality, differential cohomology makes sense for instance also in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
            where it subsumes structures such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+line+2-bundles"} super line 2-bundles
            and more generally
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Super+Gerbes"} higher super gerbes
            with connection, which naturally appear as differential refinements of the geometric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"} twists
            of (differential)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tmf"} tmf
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+differential+cohomology"} ordinary differential cohomology
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
